 "drivingQuestion": "Is the mishna's thirty-day ruling not obvious?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "the ruling teaches the term is a binding condition",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "kiddushin.60b.13",
   "daf": "60b",
   "sentenceId": 12,
   "he": "״עַל מְנָת שֶׁאֶתֵּן לְךָ מִכָּאן וְכוּ׳״. פְּשִׁיטָא! מַהוּ דְּתֵימָא: לָאו תְּנָאָה הוּא, וּלְזָרוֹזֵיהּ קָאָמְרָ[ה], קָא מַשְׁמַע לַן.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: With regard to one says to a woman: You are hereby betrothed to me «on the condition that I will give you» a particular sum of money «from now» and until thirty days have elapsed, if he gives her the money within the allotted time she is betrothed. The Gemara questions the need for this ruling: It is «obvious» that this is the case. The Gemara explains: This ruling is necessary, «lest you say» that the specified time period «is not a» proper «condition, and he said» so only «to motivate himself» to prove the sincerity of his intentions. The mishna therefore «teaches us» that the time period is an essential part of his condition, and if he fails to give her the money within this period the betrothal is void."
  }
 ],
